commit 21a6f0cbd3bef24d47e488a48cc9cb4d0bf97743
Author: Yuki Fushinuki <oimo2338@gmail.com>
Date:   Wed Aug 26 08:55:29 2026 +0900

    feat(v4): let z.nanoid() take a custom length (#4004)

    A nanoid is 21 characters only because that is the generator's default `size`; `nanoid(10)` and `nanoid(64)` are ordinary documented calls, so the fixed `{21}` regex rejected ids real producers emit. Only the count varies — the default alphabet is exactly the 64 characters the existing class already spells — so the length rides on the def and the character class is untouched.

    Reworked from the original approach in this PR, which took a positional `nanoid(64)` on `z.string().nanoid()` alone. Dropping `length` from the omit list on the params type reaches every surface with no new signatures: `z.nanoid({ length })`, `zod/mini`, and the deprecated string method all work, matching how `z.uuid({ version })` and `z.url({ hostname })` already parameterize a format.

    The default keeps the shared regex literal rather than constructing one per schema, so an ordinary `z.nanoid()` costs no extra memory. A length that is not a positive integer now throws instead of building a regex whose braces are literal text.

    Zod 3 is in maintenance mode and is intentionally left untouched.

    Refs #3954

